EtherNAT driver for FreeMiNT

The SMSC LAN91C111 on the EtherNAT board for the CT60.

The driver is a port of the Linux smc91x driver
(drivers/net/ethernet/smsc/smc91x.c and smc91x.h) with the EtherNAT platform
glue of arch/m68k/atari. Names are kept as in Linux so that fixes can be
carried over. See the comments at the top of ethernat.c for what was left
out and for the two places where Linux mechanisms were mapped onto
FreeMiNT ones.

Installation

Copy ethernat.xif to the MiNT directory. The MAC address is read from
ETHERNAT.INF in the same place or in the root of the boot drive: twelve
hex digits, optionally separated by ':' or '-', for example

	00:20:43:58:78:3c

Without the file the driver keeps whatever the chip's address registers
hold, and if that is not a valid address it makes up a locally
administered one and says so at boot. The address can also be set while
the interface is down with

	ifconfig en0 hwaddr 00:20:43:58:78:3c

It is not stored anywhere, so put it in the network startup script or in
ETHERNAT.INF.

Link state and negotiated speed/duplex are reported through the kernel's
alert channel when they change.

Hardware notes

The 91C111 registers are at 0x80000000. Byte 0x80000023 is the EtherNAT
CPLD control register: bit 0 LAN half clock mode (left as found), bit 1
LAN interrupt enable, bit 2 USB interrupt enable (the USB driver's), bits
6 and 7 the LEDs. The LAN interrupt arrives on vector 0xc4. The USB
part of the board is handled by the driver in sys/usb/src.km/ucd/ethernat.
